 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: What&#x27;s next for Israel 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Benjamin Netanyahu addresses supporters at an election night rally. Photo: Jalaa Marey/AFP via Getty Images Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's electoral coalition has opened a sizable lead over Gadi Eisenkot's opposition bloc with 85% of the vote counted, Axios' Barak Ravid reports from Tel Aviv. 

 Still, neither bloc will clear a 61-seat majority. That sets the stage for weeks of wrangling over the country's next government. 🗳️ Netanyahu will emerge from the election down but not out. 

 His bloc is on pace to secure 53 seats — 11 fewer than he currently commands. Eisenkot's bloc trails with 48 seats, leaving both men without a clear mandate to lead the government.

 Eisenkot could theoretically swear in a government with the support of Arab parties — which are on track to secure a record 19 seats — yet that's highly unlikely. Gadi Eisenkot waves an Israeli flag at an election night rally. Photo: Tomer Neuberg/Middle East Images/AFP via Getty Images 🤳 President Trump told Axios in a brief interview today that at first he received "horror reports" about Netanyahu's prospects. But, Trump added, "he's in pretty good shape now."

 Trump said: "I heard my endorsement made a big difference for Bibi." Asked whether he'll get involved in efforts to form a Netanyahu-led unity government, Trump said he hadn't thought about it. 

 He said: "I will take a look." 🪖 What's next: There are 500,000 votes left to count, from soldiers, diplomats, prisoners and others who voted outside their designated area.

 They could slightly shift the blocs' seat totals, or the allotment of seats within each bloc. But they are unlikely to change the overall balance of power. Keep reading . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 🎢 Theme park visits slide 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : TEA Global Experience. Table: Alex Fitzpatrick/Axios Attendance at most of the country's 10 busiest theme parks fell between 2024 and 2025, per closely watched estimates.

 Traffic at Disney's Magic Kingdom and EPCOT in Florida fell 0.2%, while Universal Studios Hollywood visits fell by 8%, according to the annual TEA Global Experience Index. 🎡 Some theme park fans may have chosen to spend their money and time off last year at the new Universal Epic Universe, which opened in 2025 and drew 4 million visitors.

 TEA's report also points to a drop in Canadian visitors to the U.S. amid political tensions and a weaker Canadian dollar. 🎸 Stunning stat: Visits to Dollywood in Pigeon Forge, Tenn., hit 3.4 million, up a whopping 8.1%.

